 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ for quantization and dequantization, where $N$ is the number of voxels. The operations are simple arithmetic and clamping.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot b)$ bits, where $b$ is the bit depth. For 8-bit quantization of 32-bit data, memory usage is reduced by 4x [S1, S2, unofficial].
-- **Measured Performance:** Achieves a 4:1 compression ratio for 32-bit to 8-bit conversion [S1, S2, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Simple to implement, fast, predictable error distribution for uniformly distributed data.
-- **Limitations:** Poor performance for data with wide dynamic ranges or non-uniform distributions, as important features may be lost if the quantization range is not carefully chosen [S1, S2, unofficial].
-- **When to Use:** When data is uniformly distributed or when the range of interest is well-known and narrow.
-- **When Not to Use:** When data has a wide dynamic range and important features are in narrow ranges (e.g., turbulence flow), where non-uniform or adaptive quantization is preferred [S1, S2, unofficial].
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+*   Time: $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels. The operation involves a single pass to find min/max and a single pass to quantize.
+*   Memory: Reduces storage by a factor of 4 for 32-bit to 8-bit conversion. No additional memory overhead for the quantization process itself [S1, S2, unofficial].
+
+**Measured Performance:**
+*   Achieves a 4:1 compression ratio for 32-bit to 8-bit data [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   Performance is hardware-independent as it is a simple arithmetic operation.
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+*   Strengths: Simple to implement, fast, predictable memory usage.
+*   Limitations: Ineffective for data with wide dynamic ranges (e.g., turbulence flow) where important features may become invisible if uniform spacing is used without care [S1, S2, unofficial]. Non-uniform or adaptive quantization is preferred in such cases to minimize distortion error [S1, S2, unofficial].
+
+**When to Use:**
+*   Use when data distribution is relatively uniform or when the full range of values is equally important.
+*   Do not use when data has a wide dynamic range with critical features in narrow intervals, as these will be poorly resolved [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Uniform quantization is a well-studied area in signal processing and data compression [S1, S2, unofficial]. In the context of volume rendering, its impact has not been as carefully studied as other aspects, but it is recognized as the simplest lossy compression method [S1, S2, unofficial]. The method is often used as a baseline for comparison with more advanced techniques like non-uniform and adaptive quantization [S1, S2, unofficial]. The sources cite the work of NASA and DTIC in 1998 regarding efficient encoding and rendering of time-varying volume data [S1, S2, unofficial].
+Uniform quantization is described as the simplest lossy compression method in the context of volume rendering [S1, S2, unofficial]. It was analyzed in the context of efficient encoding and rendering of time-varying volume data, highlighting the impact of quantization on visualization quality [S1, S2, unofficial]. The method is a standard baseline against which non-uniform and adaptive quantizers are compared [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
